// idea_to_pr.dot
//
// Simplified derivative of pipelines/idea_to_shipped/idea_to_shipped.dot.
// Same "idea -> plan -> build" arc, but with the ceremony that pipeline
// carries deliberately stripped down:
//
//   - NO PlanConfidenceCheck / PlanApproval human gate. Plan always flows
//     straight into Implement. If the plan is under-specified, that surfaces
//     as implementation friction, not an up-front review step nobody uses.
//   - ONE combined Implement step plus an independent SelfEvaluate (judge)
//     step instead of the three-agent implementer -> verifier ->
//     quality-reviewer pipeline in build_loop.dot.
//   - Completion is gated by RUBRIC ACHIEVEMENT, not a round count, and the
//     rubric itself follows the weighted, evidence-based methodology in
//     https://github.com/microsoft/amplifier-bundle-evaluation/blob/main/context/methodology/rubric-design.md
//     -- see the Plan node below. A round counter still exists
//     (CheckFixRounds) as a safety BACKSTOP against a genuinely
//     non-convergent loop (default 2 rounds) -- it is not how "done" is
//     decided, and if it trips the pipeline reports failure honestly
//     (build.verdict=escalated) rather than silently declaring success.
//   - Stops after delivering a PR. No MergeGate / DeployGate here -- the
//     normal GitHub PR review is the human gate, and the pipelines/ship_ready
//     pipeline (triggered by the PR itself via GitHub Actions) handles
//     preview + prod deploy automatically. This pipeline's only job is
//     idea -> plan -> build -> open PR.
//
// FLATTENED (single file, no subgraphs): earlier revisions of this pipeline
// delegated the build/verify loop and the commit/push/PR sequence to
// subgraphs/build_verify.dot and subgraphs/deliver_pr.dot via `shape=folder`
// nodes. Per docs/RUBRIC.md's own doctrine, a pipeline's cycles are exactly
// what make it an attractor rather than a flowchart -- but a `folder`
// delegation hides those cycles a level down, so looking at just this file
// used to show a pure DAG (Start -> ... -> Implement -> DeliverPR -> Report
// -> Exit) with no visible loop at all, even though the real convergence
// loop (Implement -> SelfEvaluate -> Fix -> SelfEvaluate, bounded by
// CheckFixRounds) lived one level down in build_verify.dot. Flattening
// everything into this one file makes that loop -- and the linear
// commit/push/PR delivery sequence that follows it -- visible directly at
// the root level. No behavior changed: every node/prompt/edge below is
// unchanged from the subgraph versions, just inlined and rewired.
//
// PLAN LIKE KEN: the Plan node below is written in the spirit of
// plan-like-ken -- "human attention is the most sacred resource, do not
// waste it" -- extended to the rubric itself. A rubric that only checks
// whether files exist and commands exit 0 is dead weight: modern agents
// clear that bar by default. The rubric has to say something about the
// difference between "technically works" and "a user would actually be
// satisfied with this" -- so criteria are written and judged from a USER's
// vantage point (does this behave the way someone using the feature would
// expect?), not an engineer's checklist (did the file get written?).
//
// Verification Driven Development still supplies the EVIDENCE-GATHERING
// method underneath the rubric -- Plan assigns each task ONE of exactly
// three verification tiers (narrower than idea_to_shipped's five-tier
// ladder), in this order of preference:
//
//   1. Unit tests (pytest / vitest / whatever the repo already uses) for
//      LIBRARY code -- pure functions, data transforms, business logic with
//      no DOM / network / process boundary.
//   2. A lightweight jsdom + node test environment (vitest/jest with
//      environment=jsdom, or an equivalent node-based DOM shim) for
//      INTEGRATION tests -- component wiring, event handlers, state
//      management, anything that touches a simulated DOM but doesn't need a
//      real browser engine.
//   3. Real browser-based verification (playwright-cli / browser-tester)
//      ONLY for the genuinely hardest-to-verify things: focus management,
//      keyboard navigation, accessibility (ARIA roles/labels, screen-reader
//      behavior), visual layout truth. This tier should be the EXCEPTION,
//      not the default -- most tasks should resolve at tier 1 or 2.
//
// These tiers feed the rubric's evidence-gathering "steps" (what to run
// before judging), but the rubric's criteria themselves ask whether a user
// would be satisfied, not just whether the command exited 0.
//
// RUBRIC METHODOLOGY / CONTRACT: a rubric is a flat set of named criteria,
// each with a point value (relative weight) and an OBSERVABLE question,
// scored by a judge (SelfEvaluate) that gathers real evidence first. Plan
// writes this contract to .resolve/plan/rubric.json, read by both Implement
// and SelfEvaluate:
//   {
//     "steps": "<ordered evidence-gathering plan -- what to run, read, or
//               actually try as a user before judging any criterion,
//               referencing the VDD tier commands from the plan doc>",
//     "pass_threshold": 0.85,
//     "criteria": {
//       "<snake_case_name>": {
//         "points": <int>,
//         "task": <N, or null for an overall/cross-cutting criterion>,
//         "description": "<observable, user-facing question -- prefixed
//                          with 'CRITICAL:' for the 2-3 heavy-weighted
//                          criteria that actually separate good work from
//                          competent-looking-but-shallow work>"
//       },
//       ...
//     }
//   }
// SelfEvaluate scores each criterion 0..points (partial credit per the
// description's own rules, not an invented scale), sums points_awarded /
// total points across all criteria, and compares against pass_threshold.
//
// A "loop" here means one full Implement -> SelfEvaluate -> Fix ->
// SelfEvaluate cycle -- NOT a conversational turn (a single loop can span
// many turns). Plan is required to write rubric criteria narrow enough that
// a well-formed task typically clears its full points on loop 1, worst-case
// converges by loop 2 (one Fix pass). If CheckFixRounds's backstop is
// regularly tripping, that's a signal the RUBRIC/TASKS were too loose or
// too broad going in -- fix the plan's task decomposition, don't just raise
// $max_fix_rounds to paper over it. CheckFixRounds is an explicit context
// counter, NOT max_retries -- max_retries only bounds a single node's own
// execution attempts (e.g. transient LLM errors), it does not limit how
// many times the graph loops back through an edge.
//
// Context inputs:
//   $design            - a design blurb from a prior /think-like-ken session
//   $task              - short task/feature name
//   $repo              - target repo slug (owner/name) for the PR
//   $max_fix_rounds    - safety-backstop cap on fix iterations (default 2)
//                        -- NOT the completion mechanism, see CheckFixRounds.
//
// Context outputs:
//   design.design_doc     - path to the design doc written by AcceptDesign
//   plan.plan_doc         - path to the implementation plan written by Plan
//   plan.rubric           - path to the rubric written by Plan (.resolve/plan/rubric.json)
//   build.verdict         - final "rubric_met" | "escalated"
//   build.fix_rounds_used - integer count of fix rounds consumed
//   build.rubric_result   - path to the final .resolve/verify/rubric_result.json
//   build.rubric_score    - final weighted score (0.0-1.0) achieved
//   delivery.pr_url       - PR URL, set by ExtractPRUrl ("" if delivery failed)
//   delivery.result       - "opened" | "failed"

digraph idea_to_pr {
  graph [
    label="Idea to PR -- Lite (VDD evidence + weighted user-facing rubric, no plan-approval gate)",
    goal="$task",
    rankdir=LR,
    default_max_retry=2,
    default_fidelity="summary:high",
    model_stylesheet="* { llm_model: claude-sonnet-4-6; llm_provider: anthropic; }"
  ];

  Start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"];
  Exit  [shape=Msquare,  label="Exit"];

  // Idempotency: CheckFixRounds increments a persistent .resolve/fix_state.json
  // counter with no reset of its own. Per docs/RUBRIC.md's idempotency
  // doctrine, re-running this pipeline against a working directory that
  // still has a stale fix_state.json from a prior invocation (e.g. a reused
  // CI checkout) would start the fix-round backstop already elevated,
  // tripping the escalation path prematurely on a run that hasn't actually
  // earned it. Clearing it here makes every fresh pipeline run start from a
  // clean fix-round budget regardless of what a previous run left behind.
  ResetFixState [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Reset Fix State",
    tool_command="rm -f .resolve/fix_state.json && printf 'reset'"
  ];

  // --- Phase 1: Accept an already-brainstormed design (same as idea_to_shipped) ---
  AcceptDesign [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="summary:high",
    label="Accept Design (from prior /think-like-ken)",
    prompt="You are given an already-validated design blurb in $design -- the output of a human's /think-like-ken session, done BEFORE this pipeline was invoked. Do NOT re-derive, re-explore, or second-guess the design. Simply write it as-is (light formatting only) to docs/plans/<date>-<topic>-design.md using the real design doc header (goal, chosen approach, architecture, components, data flow, error handling, testing strategy, open questions -- leave any section blank/'not specified' if $design doesn't cover it, do not fabricate). Print the single line: design_accepted"
  ];

  // --- Phase 2: Plan Like Ken -- three-tier VDD evidence hierarchy for HOW
  // to gather proof, PLUS a weighted, user-facing rubric (points, CRITICAL
  // markers, observable-from-a-user's-seat criteria) for WHAT counts as
  // actually done. This rubric -- not a round count -- is what SelfEvaluate
  // judges against. ---
  Plan [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="full",
    label="Plan Like Ken (VDD evidence + weighted rubric)",
    prompt="Read docs/plans/<date>-<topic>-design.md. Human attention is the most sacred resource -- do not waste it, and do not waste the pipeline's effort on a rubric that only checks the obvious. This is Verification Driven Development: the question for every task is never 'does a test pass?' -- it's 'does this actually work, the way a user experiencing this feature would judge it?' Break the design into bite-sized (2-5 min each) tasks, ONE logical unit of work each.\n\nSTEP A -- EVIDENCE METHOD per task: classify each task and assign EXACTLY ONE of these three verification tiers, in order of preference -- pick the cheapest tier that actually proves the claim: (1) UNIT TEST for library code -- pure functions, data transforms, business logic with no DOM/network/process boundary. (2) LIGHTWEIGHT JSDOM + NODE TEST ENV for integration tests -- component wiring, event handlers, state, anything touching a simulated DOM but not needing a real browser. This is the default for UI logic not tier-1-eligible. (3) REAL BROWSER VERIFICATION (playwright-cli / browser-tester) ONLY for focus management, keyboard navigation, accessibility, visual layout truth -- the exception, never the default. CRITICAL COMMAND-CORRECTNESS RULE FOR TIERS 1 AND 2: if the verification command is vitest (or any watch-mode-by-default test runner), it MUST be invoked in single-run/CI mode -- vitest's own CLI default is watch mode, which never exits and would hang the pipeline waiting for a process that never terminates. Always write the command as `vitest run` (or `vitest --run`), NEVER a bare `vitest` or `npm test`/`pnpm test`/`yarn test` unless that script itself already passes --run or sets CI=true internally -- check the repo's package.json test script and if it doesn't already force non-watch mode, write the exact non-watch invocation instead of relying on it. The same applies to any other watch-mode-by-default runner (e.g. jest --watch is opt-in so jest is fine bare, but always double check against the actual tool's documented default before writing the command). For EACH task write: exact file paths, complete copy-pasteable implementation, the static analysis command (always first), which tier applies and why, and the exact command to run for that tier (confirmed non-watch/non-blocking). Write docs/plans/<date>-<feature>-implementation.md with the standard structure (Goal/Architecture/Tech Stack/Verification approach, then Task N: Files/Implementation/Static Analysis/Verification Tier/Verification Command/Commit).\n\nSTEP B -- WEIGHTED RUBRIC (the real definition of done, following https://github.com/microsoft/amplifier-bundle-evaluation rubric-design methodology): before writing a single criterion, name the 2-3 things that would separate a genuinely good result on THIS feature from a competent-looking but shallow one -- a subtle requirement buried in the design that a skimming implementer would miss, a behavior only visible if you actually use the feature the way a user would, or a failure mode you can already picture (e.g. 'the error message technically appears but doesn't tell the user what to actually do'). These become your heavy-weighted, CRITICAL: criteria. Then for EVERY criterion: (1) phrase it as an OBSERVABLE QUESTION a judge can answer by looking at or using the artifact -- 'Does the README include (a) install steps (b) the exact run command (c) expected output?' not 'is the README good?'; 'Does submitting the form with an empty required field show an inline error naming which field is missing, not just a generic failure banner?' not 'does validation work?'. If you cannot phrase a criterion as something concrete and pointable, find the observable property underneath it or cut it. (2) THINK LIKE A USER, NOT AN ENGINEER, for both writing and later judging -- the question is never 'did the function get called' but 'would someone using this feature actually get what they came for, notice if it broke, or be confused by the result'. (3) Cut any criterion every implementation will trivially pass (file exists, script exits 0) -- those are gates that belong in the VDD evidence steps, not point-bearing rubric entries. (4) Allocate points by discriminating power: the 2-3 criteria from above should hold roughly HALF the total points combined; trivial-but-required checks get small slices. Mark heavy criteria with a 'CRITICAL:' prefix in their description. (5) Where partial credit is possible, spell out exactly how it's earned in the description (e.g. 'full credit for handling all 3 listed edge cases, 33% credit per case if some but not all are handled') so the judge isn't inventing a scale. (6) Write a short 'steps' evidence-gathering plan alongside the criteria -- what to run, read, or actually try as a user before scoring, mapped to the VDD tiers from Step A -- so every criterion has something concrete backing its judgment; gate on failure explicitly ('if the script errors or times out, score 0 for every criterion in this task's evidence'). Write .resolve/plan/rubric.json: {\\\"steps\\\": \\\"<ordered evidence-gathering plan: what to run/read/try, referencing the VDD tier commands from the plan doc>\\\", \\\"pass_threshold\\\": 0.85, \\\"criteria\\\": {\\\"<snake_case_name>\\\": {\\\"points\\\": <int>, \\\"task\\\": <N or null for an overall/cross-cutting criterion>, \\\"description\\\": \\\"<observable, user-facing question; prefix with 'CRITICAL:' if this is one of the 2-3 heavy-weighted ones>\\\"}, ...}}. Do not let this become mechanical busywork disconnected from what the feature is actually for -- if a criterion wouldn't matter to someone using the result, it doesn't belong.\n\nSTEP C -- REFERENCE THE RUBRIC FROM THE PLAN DOC: since the rubric lives in its own file (rubric.json), don't leave docs/plans/<date>-<feature>-implementation.md silent about it. Under each Task N section, add a short 'Acceptance' subsection listing that task's criterion name(s), points, and one-line description verbatim from rubric.json, plus the line 'Full rubric: .resolve/plan/rubric.json'. Do the same under an 'Overall Acceptance' section near the top for the cross-cutting criteria. A human skimming the plan doc alone should understand what 'done' means without having to open the JSON separately.\n\nSTEP D -- TIGHTNESS CHECK: a rubric criterion this pipeline can't realistically earn full points on in 1-2 full implement+verify loops (a 'loop' is one complete Implement -> SelfEvaluate -> Fix -> SelfEvaluate cycle, NOT a conversational turn) means the underlying TASK is too big or too ambiguous -- split it further rather than writing a loose criterion and hoping the fix loop absorbs the gap. Every criterion must be answerable using only what's already pinned down in the design doc.\n\nPrint the single line: plan_done"
  ];

  // --- Phase 3: Build, gated by weighted rubric achievement (not a round
  // count), with a safety-backstop fix loop. THE LOOP: Implement ->
  // SelfEvaluate -> CheckRubric -> [fail] CheckFixRounds -> Fix ->
  // SelfEvaluate (loop_restart) -- visible right here at the root level. ---
  // Implements the plan's tasks and gathers the evidence rubric.json's
  // "steps" call for, but does NOT self-declare pass/fail or award its own
  // points -- that judgment belongs entirely to the independent
  // SelfEvaluate step below, which doesn't trust Implement's own account.
  Implement [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="full",
    label="Implement",
    prompt="Read docs/plans/<date>-<feature>-implementation.md and .resolve/plan/rubric.json (including its 'steps' evidence plan). For EACH task not yet marked complete, in order: (1) run static analysis FIRST and fix any pre-existing issues; (2) implement exactly what's specified -- no extra features, no 'while I'm here' improvements; (3) run static analysis again; (4) run the VDD verification command the plan assigned this task's tier (unit test / jsdom+node integration test / real browser -- do NOT substitute a cheaper check, and do NOT reach for a browser if the plan's tier is unit or jsdom). If that command is vitest (or any other watch-mode-by-default runner) and the plan wrote a bare `vitest`/`npm test`, do NOT run it as-is -- run `vitest run` (or add `--run`) instead, since vitest's own default is watch mode and a bare invocation will hang forever waiting for a process that never exits. Never run a test command in watch mode in this pipeline. Also actually exercise the feature the way rubric.json's 'steps' describes for this task's criteria -- if a criterion is about what a user would experience, go look at or try that, don't infer it from the code. Document the ACTUAL output/behavior you saw verbatim -- do not summarize or paraphrase it. (5) Commit atomically: 'feat: [what this does]'. Append your result to docs/plans/<feature>-status.md under this task's heading with the exact static-analysis output, the exact verification command + output, and what you observed when exercising the feature per the rubric's steps. After all tasks are attempted, also work through rubric.json's overall 'steps' (e.g. full test suite, static analysis clean, any cross-cutting user-facing check) and document that output too. Do NOT self-score against the rubric and do NOT declare victory -- just report what you did and what actually happened. Print the single line: implement_done"
  ];

  // The judge. Adversarial and independent: gathers/re-gathers evidence
  // per rubric.json's "steps", then scores EVERY criterion against that
  // evidence -- never against Implement's status write-up alone. Thinks
  // like a user experiencing the result, not an engineer confirming a
  // command exited 0.
  SelfEvaluate [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="full",
    label="Self-Evaluate (judge, weighted rubric)",
    prompt="Read .resolve/plan/rubric.json in full (its 'steps', 'pass_threshold', and every criterion) and docs/plans/<feature>-status.md. First, work through rubric.json's 'steps' yourself right now -- re-run commands, read the actual files, and where a criterion is about user-facing behavior, actually use the feature the way a real user would rather than reading the code and assuming. If status.md's account looks incomplete, inconsistent, or suspiciously clean anywhere, that is exactly when to re-verify directly rather than trust it. Then, for EACH criterion in rubric.json: think like a user, not an engineer -- award points_awarded from 0 up to that criterion's max points based on what the evidence actually shows, using any partial-credit rule stated in the criterion's own description (do not invent your own scale). A 'CRITICAL:' criterion failing should cost real points, not a token deduction. Do not award points for something merely claimed in status.md without evidence backing it. Do not invent criteria that aren't in rubric.json, and do not skip any that are. Compute overall_score = sum(points_awarded) / sum(points) across all criteria. Write .resolve/verify/rubric_result.json: {\\\"overall_score\\\": <float 0-1>, \\\"pass_threshold\\\": <copied from rubric.json>, \\\"results\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": \\\"<criterion id>\\\", \\\"points\\\": <max>, \\\"points_awarded\\\": <awarded>, \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"<what evidence you checked and why this many points>\\\"}, ...]}. Print the single line: rubric_verdict=pass if overall_score >= pass_threshold, or rubric_verdict=fail otherwise, followed on the next line by rubric_score=<overall_score>."
  ];

  // Reads .resolve/verify/rubric_result.json directly rather than grepping
  // $last_response for the "rubric_verdict=" marker line. SelfEvaluate runs
  // under this graph's default_fidelity="summary:high" (idea_to_pr.dot),
  // and per docs/primer.md's foot-gun card #3, last_response truncates to
  // 200 chars except under fidelity=full -- SelfEvaluate's prompt asks for
  // a long rubric write-up with the verdict marker printed LAST, so
  // truncation could silently drop the very line this gate greps for, and
  // it would then always fail through to CheckFixRounds regardless of the
  // real verdict. That failed safe (never falsely claimed pass) but could
  // burn fix-loop budget on a rubric that genuinely passed. Reading the
  // file SelfEvaluate already writes sidesteps last_response entirely --
  // same pattern build_loop.dot's VerdictGate uses for verdict.json.
  CheckRubric [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Check Rubric Verdict",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nset -e\npython3 -c \"import json,os,sys; p='.resolve/verify/rubric_result.json'; d=json.load(open(p)) if os.path.exists(p) else {}; sys.exit(0 if d.get('overall_score', 0) >= d.get('pass_threshold', 1) else 1)\" && printf 'pass' || printf 'fail'"
  ];

  // Safety backstop ONLY -- not the completion mechanism. Default 2
  // matches the "typically 1-2 loops per task" expectation a tight,
  // well-weighted rubric should produce: attempt 1 (Implement's own
  // pass), then at most one Fix pass. If the rubric was scoped correctly,
  // this rarely trips; if it trips often, the fix is tighter task/rubric
  // decomposition in Plan, not a larger number here.
  CheckFixRounds [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Check Fix Rounds (safety backstop)",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nset -e\nmkdir -p .resolve\ncount=$(python3 -c \"import json,os;p='.resolve/fix_state.json';d=json.load(open(p)) if os.path.exists(p) else {'count':0};d['count']+=1;json.dump(d,open(p,'w'));print(d['count'])\")\nmax=${max_fix_rounds:-2}\nif [ \"$count\" -le \"$max\" ]; then printf 'fix_ok'; else printf 'fix_exhausted'; fi"
  ];

  Fix [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="full",
    label="Fix (address lowest-scoring rubric criteria)",
    prompt="Read .resolve/verify/rubric_result.json. Prioritize any criterion whose description was marked 'CRITICAL:' and scored below full points, then work through the rest by largest points-lost (points - points_awarded). For each, fix the SPECIFIC gap the reasoning field points to -- no broad rewrite, address exactly what's short. Re-run that criterion's evidence per rubric.json's 'steps' and confirm the fresh result actually earns more points before moving to the next one. Update docs/plans/<feature>-status.md with what changed and the fresh evidence. Print the single line: fix_attempted"
  ];

// Split into two literal-outcome nodes rather than one shared node that
  // relies on a positional "$1" shell argument -- nothing in this graph
  // ever passes an argument into a bare tool_command invocation, so that
  // argument would always be empty, making build.verdict always "" and
  // guaranteeing "no matching edge" in the parent graph on EVERY run,
  // regardless of whether the rubric actually passed or the backstop
  // tripped. Each node below hard-codes its own literal verdict instead
  // and computes the shared rubric_score itself.
  ExtractVerdictMet [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Extract Verdict (rubric met)",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nset -e\ncount=$(python3 -c \"import json,os;p='.resolve/fix_state.json';print(json.load(open(p))['count'] if os.path.exists(p) else 0)\")\nscore=$(python3 -c \"import json,os;p='.resolve/verify/rubric_result.json';print(json.load(open(p)).get('overall_score', 0) if os.path.exists(p) else 0)\")\nprintf '{\"build.verdict\": \"rubric_met\", \"build.fix_rounds_used\": %s, \"build.rubric_result\": \".resolve/verify/rubric_result.json\", \"build.rubric_score\": %s}' \"$count\" \"$score\"",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  ExtractVerdictEscalated [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Extract Verdict (escalated)",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nset -e\ncount=$(python3 -c \"import json,os;p='.resolve/fix_state.json';print(json.load(open(p))['count'] if os.path.exists(p) else 0)\")\nscore=$(python3 -c \"import json,os;p='.resolve/verify/rubric_result.json';print(json.load(open(p)).get('overall_score', 0) if os.path.exists(p) else 0)\")\nprintf '{\"build.verdict\": \"escalated\", \"build.fix_rounds_used\": %s, \"build.rubric_result\": \".resolve/verify/rubric_result.json\", \"build.rubric_score\": %s}' \"$count\" \"$score\"",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  // --- Phase 4: Deliver the PR (commit, push, verify the push landed, open
  // PR). Linear once the rubric is met -- no loop needed here, CheckPush is
  // a one-shot evidence gate, not a convergence loop. ---
  CommitPush [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="full",
    label="Commit + Push",
    prompt="Stage all changes, write a conventional commit message summarizing the work (reference .resolve/design/design.md and .ai/plan.md for context), commit, and push the current branch to origin. After pushing, run: git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD > .resolve/delivery/push_status.txt && echo pushed >> .resolve/delivery/push_status.txt. Print the single line: pushed"
  ];

  CheckPush [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Check Push",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nset -e\nif [ -f .resolve/delivery/push_status.txt ] && tail -1 .resolve/delivery/push_status.txt | grep -q pushed; then\n  printf 'pushed'\nelse\n  printf 'push_failed'\nfi"
  ];

  OpenPR [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="full",
    label="Open PR",
    prompt="Open a REAL pull request on GitHub (https://github.com/$repo) for the pushed branch against $repo's default branch -- use the gh CLI (`gh pr create --repo $repo ...`) or the GitHub REST API directly (POST https://api.github.com/repos/$repo/pulls with GH_TOKEN), NOT any other git host or a simulated/local PR. Write a PR description summarizing the design, the task list completed, and verification evidence. Write the resulting real github.com PR URL to .resolve/delivery/pr_url.txt. Print the single line: {\"pr_url\": \"<url>\"} as JSON."
  ];

  // Extracts the URL OpenPR claims to have opened -- NOT yet trusted as
  // delivery.result="opened". See CheckPROpened below: an LLM node can
  // print a plausible-looking PR URL, or gh/curl can fail after emitting
  // output the agent misreads, without a PR actually existing on GitHub.
  ExtractPRUrl [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Extract PR URL (claimed)",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nset -e\nurl=$(cat .resolve/delivery/pr_url.txt)\nprintf '{\"delivery.pr_url\": \"%s\"}' \"$url\"",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  // Independent verification that OpenPR's claimed side effect (a real PR
  // on GitHub) actually happened -- deliberately uses only gh CLI / curl
  // against the GitHub REST API, NOT any Resolve-platform-specific tool
  // (e.g. the worker-container `create-pr` helper). This pipeline is
  // fetched and run via a git+https:// URL by any dot-graph-compatible
  // engine, not exclusively Resolve's own worker containers -- baking in a
  // Resolve-only binary would silently break portability to other
  // runtimes. gh/curl + GH_TOKEN is the same portable toolset every other
  // pipeline in this repo already assumes (see pr_review.dot,
  // pr-review-exhaustive.dot). This mirrors CheckPush's evidence-based
  // pattern one hop further: don't let delivery.result say "opened" on a
  // self-report alone -- confirm the PR is real via a second, independent
  // API call before Report is allowed to claim success.
  CheckPROpened [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Verify PR Actually Opened",
    tool_command="#!/bin/sh\nnum=$(printf '%s' \"$delivery_pr_url\" | grep -oE '[0-9]+$' 2>/dev/null || true)\nif [ -n \"$num\" ] && gh pr view \"$num\" --repo \"$repo\" --json state >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  printf 'verified'\nelif [ -n \"$num\" ] && curl -sf -H \"Authorization: token ${GH_TOKEN}\" \"https://api.github.com/repos/$repo/pulls/$num\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  printf 'verified'\nelse\n  printf 'not_verified'\nfi"
  ];

  // Only set delivery.result="opened" once CheckPROpened has independently
  // confirmed the PR is real -- this is the only node allowed to claim
  // delivery success.
  MarkOpened [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Mark Opened (verified)",
    tool_command="printf '{\"delivery.result\": \"opened\"}'",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  Failed [
    shape=parallelogram, label="Mark Failed",
    tool_command="printf '{\"delivery.result\": \"failed\"}'",
    parse_json="true"
  ];

  Report [
    shape=box, goal_gate=true, fidelity="summary:high",
    label="Report",
    prompt="Summarize the run: design (docs/plans/<date>-<topic>-design.md), rubric (.resolve/plan/rubric.json), fix rounds used (${build.fix_rounds_used}), final build verdict (${build.verdict}), final weighted rubric score (${build.rubric_score}), rubric evaluation detail (${build.rubric_result}), and PR (${delivery.pr_url}). If build.verdict is 'escalated' (the fix-round safety backstop tripped before the rubric's pass_threshold was met), say so plainly and list which specific criteria are still short of full points and why -- do not claim success. If delivery.result is 'failed' (push did not land), say so plainly and do not claim a PR was opened. Print the single line: reported"
  ];

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ EDGES ═══════════════════════════════════════════════

  Start        -> ResetFixState;
  ResetFixState -> AcceptDesign;
  AcceptDesign -> Plan;
  Plan         -> Implement;                                    // no PlanConfidenceCheck / PlanApproval gate

  // --- Build/verify loop (visible at root level) ---
  Implement      -> SelfEvaluate;
  SelfEvaluate   -> CheckRubric;
  CheckRubric    -> ExtractVerdictMet       [condition="context.tool.last_line=pass", weight="2", label="rubric met"];
  CheckRubric    -> CheckFixRounds          [condition="context.tool.last_line=fail", label="rubric unmet"];
  CheckFixRounds -> Fix                     [condition="context.tool.last_line=fix_ok",       label="fix round (backstop max $max_fix_rounds)"];
  CheckFixRounds -> ExtractVerdictEscalated [condition="context.tool.last_line=fix_exhausted", label="backstop tripped -- escalate"];
  Fix            -> SelfEvaluate            [loop_restart="true"];    // <-- THE LOOP

  // --- Rubric met -> deliver PR; escalated -> skip straight to Report ---
  ExtractVerdictMet       -> CommitPush [weight="2"];
  ExtractVerdictEscalated -> Report;

  // --- Delivery sequence (linear, evidence-gated, no loop needed) ---
  CommitPush    -> CheckPush;
  CheckPush     -> OpenPR       [condition="context.tool.last_line=pushed", weight="2"];
  CheckPush     -> Failed       [condition="context.tool.last_line=push_failed"];
  OpenPR        -> ExtractPRUrl;
  ExtractPRUrl  -> CheckPROpened;
  CheckPROpened -> MarkOpened   [condition="context.tool.last_line=verified", weight="2"];
  CheckPROpened -> Failed       [condition="context.tool.last_line=not_verified"];
  CheckPROpened -> Failed       [condition="outcome=fail"];  // safety net: tool_command crashed entirely (RUBRIC.md 3.2)
  MarkOpened    -> Report;
  Failed        -> Report;

  Report -> Exit;
}
